The retain cycle, as code: The $3.8B is an object that is allocated and retained forever — <code>dealloc</code> is never implemented, so the memory is never freed:

```objc
@implementation USMilitaryAid
- (instancetype)initSince1951 {
  self.total = 317_900_000_000; // ~$317.9B allocated, cumulative
  return self;
}
- (void)dealloc {
  // never called. the aid is never freed.
}
+ (NSArray *)whoNeedsIsraelToReceiveThis {
  return @[ @"Lockheed", @"Raytheon", @"Boeing", @"GeneralDynamics",
            @"AIPAC-endorsed candidates" ];
  // note: does NOT return Israel's economy
}
@end
```

The retain cycle never releases because the beneficiaries holding the reference are American — contractors and campaigns, not Israel's balance sheet.

The structural difference, as code: The global lobby runs an <code>isa</code>-swizzle: AIPAC presents as one class, but the runtime routes messages through another. And ELNET is where the swizzle is caught in the act:

```objc
// What the public sees
@interface AIPAC : AmericanCivicOrganization
- (void)expressViewsOf:(Constituency *)americanJews;
@end

// What the runtime routes through
@interface ELNET : IsraeliStateAlignedNetwork   // isa pointer exposed
- (void)shapePolicy:(Parliament *)p
       reimbursedBy:(Government *)israeliGovt;   // <-- the tell
@end
```

AIPAC keeps its <code>isa</code> pointer hidden behind the civic-organization interface and never registers under FARA. ELNET's French-Senate reimbursement is the moment the <code>isa</code> pointer became visible: a state directly paying for a legislative-influence event is the runtime class the civic label was concealing.
